592186316457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66bfc5f-3122-5a00-a1eb-aba1035e251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} :answer-field/id #uuid "c113cb3c-937d-5360-937b-d1f8edc5d5c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cffe765b-9372-5c49-a0ae-c98bc95f8843" :question/math-academy-id "q-343880" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=3 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3) &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n} &=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"}]
